  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nTonight through next Monday...The primary focus during this forecast \nperiod currently revolves around a \"limited\" concern for fire danger \non Thursday, some rain chances Wednesday as well as Sunday, and the \npotential for at to below freezing low temperatures both Saturday \nand Sunday mornings.  Despite all this, quiet weather overall will \ncontinue to reside across the forecast area during this period. \nConditions will remain warm into Wednesday ahead of a cold front \nthat'll propagate through the region overnight Wednesday into early \nThursday morning.  A southern stream disturbance ahead of this front \nwill bring some rain chances to mainly southern portions of the CWA \novernight tonight into Wednesday.  Then Wednesday night as the front \ntraverses the CWA, it could squeeze out a few showers during this \ntime.  However, the majority of the forecast area will remain dry.\n\nBehind this front on Thursday, noticeably colder drier air will \nadvect into the region.  Dew points through the day will gradually \nfall into the mid to lower 40s, with some upper 30s possible as \nwell.  Meanwhile, highs will range from the middle 60s north to \nmiddle 70s south.  With afternoon relative humidity values generally \nranging between 35-45%, northwest winds sustained around 15 mph with \ngusts around 25 mph, coupled with the extremely dry soil conditions \nand vegetation across the area, a \"limited\" threat for fire danger \nwill exist across the entire CWA.\n\nColder, but overall, dry weather will persist into the upcoming \nweekend and the beginning of next week.  High temperatures Friday \nthrough Sunday will generally range from the middle 50s to middle \n60s, but increasing into the low and middle 60s come Monday.  Lows \non the other hand will be quite chilly during this time frame. \nCurrently, the coldest temperatures during this stretch look to fall \nboth Saturday morning and Sunday morning with lows advertised to \nrange from the upper 20s middle 30s.  As eluded to in previous \ndiscussion, as we move closer in time to this weekend, we will \nhighlight the areas most likely to be impacted by these freezing \nand sub-freezing temperatures in HWO graphics.\n\nLastly, a passing disturbance late in the weekend into Monday, could \nresult in some showers across the CWA.  However, these chances are \ncurrently low and there remains a good bit of uncertainty in global \nmodel data during this time.  This is due to both differing \nsolutions in said models, as well as on a run-to-run basis. /19/",
